1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,340
My name is Oscar, and I built ReadyCounter.

2
00:00:04,180 --> 00:00:06,500
Shopping agents are already in your stores,

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:08,840
and most of them leave empty-handed.

4
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:12,540
ReadyCounter scores what the agent actually can read.

5
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:15,440
I redirected it to already 148 stores

6
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:17,780
using 18 WebMCP tools.

7
00:00:17,780 --> 00:00:19,620
So let's get your stores ready,

8
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:22,060
and I'll let my agent do the demo.

9
00:00:22,705 --> 00:00:30,130
AI shoppers arrived this year. Sessions to Shopify storefronts are up eight times year over year. Orders, nearly thirteen.

10
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:41,755
And the money turned. Last March, agent traffic converted thirty eight percent worse than everything else. This March, forty two percent better. Your worst channel became your best one in twelve months.

11
00:00:42,305 --> 00:00:56,180
Here is the catch. Four in five agent carts never reach checkout. And Shopify's own earnings call says a catalogue an agent can read converts twice as well as a page it has to scrape. That is the whole prize, and it is sitting behind a data problem.

12
00:00:56,730 --> 00:01:09,155
So we asked one hundred and forty eight real storefronts for their catalogue, the way an agent asks. Seventy sent nothing back. Sixty seven sent a feed with no barcode in it. Coverage across the field: zero percent.

13
00:01:09,705 --> 00:01:17,005
This is ReadyCounter. A merchant types their domain, and that is the whole setup. No install, no account, no code on the storefront.

14
00:01:17,555 --> 00:01:26,080
The score lands, their tile joins the field, and the barcode prints exactly as far as their data goes. For this store, and for most, that is nowhere.

15
00:01:26,630 --> 00:01:36,030
Every point on the bill is priced from a published abandonment share, and every line names its source. Six sandbox storefronts, six different ways to lose the sale.

16
00:01:36,580 --> 00:01:53,530
We did not build a catalogue. We read the ones that exist. Shopify OAuth for the merchant's own admin, Shopify's Catalog protocol for the barcodes a scrape cannot see, and Render Key Value holding every audit and every shared cart across cold starts. Three integrations, all live in this build.

17
00:01:54,080 --> 00:02:15,905
Now the proof, in Chrome with WebMCP switched on. This is not a console we wrote. Eighteen tools are registered on document dot model context, the browser's own API. The agent searches the catalogue, adds to the order, and the line is tagged agent next to the one a human added. Then prepare checkout refuses, because a captcha stands in the way, and it cites the published share that says so. It never touches a card.

18
00:02:16,455 --> 00:02:19,555
ReadyCounter. Agent commerce, now reviewable.

